y and the whole CE obstruction is carried by the f=5 summand; for n ≤ 5 the f=5 summand also has negative degree so the failure locus is empty; at n=6 the f=5 obstruction has degree exactly 0, which is the unique value making the failure condition a single Abel-Jacobi condition O_C(Γ) ≅ L rather than a positive-degree Brill-Noether condition (n ≥ 7). The identification proved for (6,6) is therefore structurally specific to the borderline degree and does not transport to neighbouring n by the same argument.\n67\t- `FND-0075` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: The finite étale S_6-torsor Conf_6 → (reduced-support open) holds on the stack of pairwise-distinct sections and fails on coarse moduli at curves whose automorphisms permute the marked points. Downstream nodes that descend a cohomology or weight computation from the ordered model to the unordered one must therefore work with stack (orbifold) cohomology; with Q-coefficients S_6-invariants and S_6-coinvariants agree, so the descent is harmless rationally, but the same descent is not available integrally and is not available on the coarse space. Any integral-coefficient or coarse-space version of a C66-TATE-* argument built on this model needs an independent justification.\n68\t- `FND-0083` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: For L=W_5⊗ω_C^{-1} of degree 6 on a smooth genus-6 curve, Riemann–Roch χ(L)=1 proves only that the full Abel–Jacobi fibre in Sym^6 is nonempty; it does not prove that the reduced-support open, or the ordered Conf_6 incidence Z_ev, is nonempty on every geometric fibre.\n69\t- `FND-0084` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: ATT-0034's localization proof requires CKgP, or an equivalent lowest-weight Tate theorem, for the CE-surjective open U. The supplied construction does not identify the spaces and maps sufficiently to apply any preservation result, and its cited CKgP dependency is scoped to M_{6,5}.\n70\t- `FND-0085` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: The finite branch morphism from the Hurwitz stack to an open of M_{0,18} does not by itself imply mixed-Tate cohomology of the Hurwitz stack or its projective-bundle open: the finite pushforward of the constant sheaf may contain non-Tate local systems. Rational averaging only preserves an already established Tate structure on the cover.\n71\t- `FND-0086` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: The dominant dimension count and named exceptional loci do not constitute an exhaustive component, stabilizer, closure, and codimension analysis of Z. Until every contributing stratum is identified, the support filtration and its Gysin inputs in degree 26 cannot be declared Tate or vanishing.\n72\t- `FND-0087` [corroborated; Relative configuration and Abel–Jacobi incidence spaces over possibly nonreduced bases.]: For the ordered-to-unordered reduced-support construction, reduction must be applied compatibly: the canonical torsor is I→I^{red-supp}, and its reduced form is I_red→(I^{red-supp})_red. Reducing only the source generally destroys flatness over nonreduced bases.\n73\t- `FND-0089` [corroborated; {'applies_to': 'attempts proving low-degree boundary-restriction kernels of H^*(overline M_{g,n}) are Tate', 'coefficients': 'Q', 'methods': ['ordered stable-arc complex', 'boundary clutching pullbacks']}]: A purely combinatorial ordered-arc insertion operator does not supply a chain homotopy on the Künneth-decorated one-edge restriction complex: without a cohomological rule for how vertex classes transform under arc insertion, and without a comparison theorem identifying the arc complex with the restriction/Čech complex for clutching maps, one cannot conclude that the simultaneous one-edge kernel P^m_{g,n} is Tate.\n74\t- `FND-0090` [corroborated; {'coefficients': 'Q', 'objects': ['P^m_{g,n}', 'boundary Gysin filtration']}]: Degree counts that invoke per-edge node/Gysin shifts analyze a different complex (Gysin/weight spectral sequence or boundary-pushforward span) from the simulta