-001', 'subproblem_id': 'C66-GEO-Z'}]: To prove that Z^{bal} is not closed in M_{6,6}, one must exhibit or otherwise establish a closure point in Z minus Z^{bal}. Openness of base-point-freeness/balancedness and one balanced example are insufficient.\n- `FND-0068` [corroborated; {'attempt_id': 'ATT-0026', 'campaign_id': 'C66-001', 'subproblem_id': 'C66-GEO-Z'}]: A degeneracy locus whose component has codimension below the generic determinantal expectation may still be locally cut out by a regular sequence of its actual codimension. A nowhere-lci conclusion requires local ideal-depth or conormal calculations not present in ATT-0026.\n- `FND-0069` [corroborated; {'attempt_id': 'ATT-0026', 'campaign_id': 'C66-001', 'subproblem_id': 'C66-GEO-Z'}]: The Petri-general fibre calculation proves a smooth 17-dimensional dominant component, but global claims about codim Z require excluding larger components over special curve loci and distinguishing component existence from purity.\n- `FND-0071` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: A low-degree diagonal statement that, at 2r=2g+n-2, only the kernel of restriction H^{2r}(\\overline{M}_{g,n})→H^{2r}(D) is Tate does not by itself make the whole group H^{2r}(\\overline{M}_{g,n}) Tate; boundary-supported classes and the full stratified contribution must still be controlled.\n- `FND-0072` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: No complete unconditional proof that H^{16}(\\overline{M}_{6,6};Q) is a direct sum of Q(-8) is supplied by ATT-0027; graph-complex/arc-contraction slogans without Aut-invariants, Gysin shifts, differentials, and higher-genus primitives do not discharge this.\n- `FND-0073` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: On any smooth genus-6 geometric fibre of the balanced tetragonal CE family, h^0(L) = 1 + h^0(M) where M := ω_C^2⊗W_5^∨ has deg M = 2(2g-2) - (4g+12-4f)|_{g=6,f=5} = 20 - 16 = 4. This follows from χ(L)=1 plus Serre duality h^1(L) = h^0(ω_C⊗L^{-1}) = h^0(ω_C^2⊗W_5^∨), and uses no part of the undischarged GAP-Z-W5D identification W_5 ≅ ω_C^2(-D). Consequently the C66-GEO-H0 stratification of the relative degree-six linear system by h^0(L) is exactly the stratification of a relative degree-4 line bundle by h^0, i.e. a g^1_4-type Brill-Noether question in degree 4 rather than degree 6, with h^0(L)=3 (fibrewise |L| ≅ P^2) precisely where h^0(M)=2.\n- `FND-0074` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: Substituting g=6 into the SRC-0004 obstruction degree 4f+n-2g-14 gives n-10 for f=4 and n-6 for f=5. Hence for all n ≤ 9 the f=4 summand vanishes identically and the whole CE obstruction is carried by the f=5 summand; for n ≤ 5 the f=5 summand also has negative degree so the failure locus is empty; at n=6 the f=5 obstruction has degree exactly 0, which is the unique value making the failure condition a single Abel-Jacobi condition O_C(Γ) ≅ L rather than a positive-degree Brill-Noether condition (n ≥ 7). The identification proved for (6,6) is therefore structurally specific to the borderline degree and does not transport to neighbouring n by the same argument.\n- `FND-0075` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: The finite étale S_6-torsor Conf_6 → (reduced-support open) holds on the stack of pairwise-distinct sections and fails on coarse moduli at curves whose automorphisms permute the marked points. Downstream nodes that descend a cohomology or weight computation from the ordered model to the unordered one must therefore work with stack (orbifold) cohomology; with Q-coefficients S_6-invariants and S_6-coinvariants agree, so the descent is harmless rationally, but the same descent is not available integrally and is not available on the coarse space. Any integral-coefficient or coarse-space version of a C66-TATE-* argument built on this model needs an independent justification.\n- `FND-0083` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: For L=W_5⊗ω_C^{-1} of degree 6 on a smooth genus-6 curve, Riemann–Roch χ(L)=1 proves only that the full Abel–Jacobi fibre in Sym^6 is nonempty; it does not prove that the reduced-support open, or the ordered Conf_6 incidence Z_ev, is nonempty on every geometric fibre.\n- `FND-0084` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: ATT-0034's localization proof requires CKgP, or an equivalent lowest-weight Tate theorem, for the CE-surjective open U. The supplied construction does not identify the spaces and maps sufficiently to apply any preservation result, and its cited CKgP dependency is scoped to M_{6,5}.\n- `FND-0085` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: The finite branch morphism from the Hurwitz stack to an open of M_{0,18} does not by itself imply mixed-Tate cohomology of the Hurwitz stack or its projective-bundle open: the finite pushforward of the constant sheaf may contain non-Tate local systems. Rational averaging only preserves an already established Tate structure on the cover.\n- `FND-0086` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: The dominant dimension count and named exceptional loci do not constitute an exhaustive component, stabilizer, closure, and codimension analysis of Z. Until every contributing stratum is identified, the support filtration and its Gysin inputs in degree 26 cannot be declared Tate or vanishing.\n- `FND-0087` [corroborated; Relative configuration and Abel–Jacobi incidence spaces over possibly nonreduced bases.]: For the ordered-to-unordered reduced-support construction, reduction must be applied compatibly: the canonical torsor is I→I^{red-supp}, and its reduced form is I_red→(I^{red-supp})_red. Reducing only the source generally destroys flatness over nonreduced bases.\n- `FND-0089` [corroborated; {'applies_to': 'attempts proving low-degree boundary-restriction kernels of H^*(overline M_{g,n}) are Tate', 'coefficients': 'Q', 'methods': ['ordered stable-arc complex', 'boundary clutching pullbacks']}]: A purely combinatorial ordered-arc insertion operator does not supply a chain homotopy on the Künneth-decorated one-edge restriction complex: